      "type": "done",
      "answer": "## AMAT Formula\n\nThe lecture defines **Average Memory Access Time (AMAT)** as:\n\n**AMAT = Hit time + Miss rate × Miss penalty**\n\nEquivalently, for a first-level cache:\n\n**AMAT = T_hit(L1) + Miss%(L1) × T(memory)**\n\nSee page 26 [S1] and page 27 [S2].\n\n### Worked example (from the lecture, page 27)\n- Cache hit time = 1 cycle\n- Miss rate = 10% = 0.1\n- Miss penalty = 300 cycles\n\nAMAT = 1 + 0.1 × 300 = **31 cycles** [S2]\n\n## Miss Penalty — Explanation\n\nThe **miss penalty** is the *additional time required to service a miss* — i.e., the time to fetch the requested block from the next lower memory level when it is not present in the current cache level [S4].\n\nMore specifically, the lecture breaks it down (page 26):\n\n- **Access time**: the latency for the memory to handle one access.\n- **Transfer time**: the latency to transfer the data block up to the higher level.\n\nSo:\n\n**Miss penalty = access time + (data size / bandwidth)** [S1]\n\nIn other words, the penalty is not just the wait for the lower-level memory to respond, but also the time to actually move the data block back up to the cache. A cache is beneficial when most accesses are fast hits and only a small fraction of accesses pay this miss penalty [S1].\n\n### Multi-level extension (page 28)\nWith multiple cache levels, the penalty is nested recursively:\n\n**AMAT = T_hit(L1) + Miss%(L1) × ( T_hit(L2) + Miss%(L2) × ( T_hit(L3) + Miss%(L3) × T(memory) ) )**\n\nThe lecture notes this reduces AMAT from 31 cycles (single level) to about 2.115 cycles, a ~14.7× speedup [S3].\n\nAll of the above is drawn directly from the current lecture (pages 25–28).",
